WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.540
The most important question you should ask about Cedric Price is why did he die so

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.000
soon?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.885
Because I think that he would have been in his element in the smart thinking about

00:00:14.885 --> 00:00:19.000
cities and planning that we have available today,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.131
which links back to his thinking about cybernetics, about the way cities work, the

00:00:25.131 --> 00:00:27.000
social aspects of cities,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:36.283
brain power and the ability to analyze things at the speed of light and to be clever

00:00:36.283 --> 00:00:37.720
about the way

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:46.215
we do things so I think that I feel sorry that he's not around now to help us

00:00:46.215 --> 00:00:47.980
evaluate what is

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:55.453
going on in relationship to new technologies and the way we run our cities yeah when

00:00:55.453 --> 00:00:56.520
I saw Cedric

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.920
for the first time, yeah, I mean, he was no doubt a star.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.240
You know, he had this rather portly carriage,

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.520
always with the cigar puffing away.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:15.400
And he was somebody who stood out from the rest.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:22.920
Generally, as I say, with his rounded, stiff, starched collars,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:32.240
knitted black tie, baggy black jumper and hush puppy shoes, very distinctive and

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:40.920
his own man very much really and that sort of long black hair that he swept

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:48.360
back. He was quite difficult to pin down, he was quite secretive, everyone

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:54.333
thought he didn't have to work because he was the heir to the fortune of the Prices

00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:55.340
Candle Factory

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:03.709
fortune but I don't believe that was actually the case. Nevertheless he would

00:02:03.709 --> 00:02:06.100
generally drive around

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:15.340
in chauffeur driven cars quite frequently. I at that stage lived in the West Country

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.440
in a small

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:23.266
village called Laycock in Wiltshire and he decided to come down and visit my family

00:02:23.266 --> 00:02:24.500
for the day and

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:30.947
he took a taxi all the way from London it's about 108 miles actually to get there

00:02:30.947 --> 00:02:32.220
taxi all the way

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.744
down taxi waited while he had lunch had a look around the village and the taxi took

00:02:36.744 --> 00:02:37.180
him back

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:45.900
again so um you know he he lived a fairly luxurious existence in in that way and he

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:46.320
made

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:57.455
really specific choices about how he ran his life obviously lunch eating and

00:02:57.455 --> 00:02:59.360
drinking were

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.700
a key part of the pleasures he got out of life.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.220
When I was editor of Building Design, for instance,

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:14.440
every Monday morning I would go at 8 o'clock

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.220
for what you call breakfast.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.240
Breakfast was a glass of brandy.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.540
His was a bit deeper than mine.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.320
I have to say I was surprised that brandy at that time

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:34.109
in the morning has a very energizing impact on you for quite a long time during the

00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:34.340
day,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:43.780
which is, but I didn't really take to it. Nevertheless, those meetings were another

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:49.907
interesting side of him of his interest in politics, his interest in gossip, his

00:03:49.907 --> 00:03:50.520
interest

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:58.540
in the way you had an impact on decision-making at the highest level

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:04.380
and made him really behind the scenes politically very powerful.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.900
To me, he was something of a mystery always.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.960
He was always found tremendously charming,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:17.920
very charming to the ladies.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:25.920
and yet there were times when he could react very angrily about things.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.820
I remember the thing that he used to get most upset was

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:33.500
if one turned up at the office without an appointment,

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.260
which he only did once,

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.940
and he liked things to be organised

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:44.060
and I'm sure many people have told you about East Grinstead

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:49.420
so that he could disappear when he wanted to.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.500
But he never actually taught me

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.780
because when I came to the AA, he wasn't teaching here.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.900
But in those days, I always think the AA had a rather better relationship

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:05.680
with practices in the area, in London,

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.420
and you could go out and forage for yourself

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.100
to find people who would support you.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:18.983
So he was really a mentor for me, particularly when I was editing Clip Kit with

00:05:18.983 --> 00:05:20.480
Jeffrey Smythe.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.300
So Jeffrey and I would go and have regular meetings with Cedric.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:33.426
And Cedric would be totally generous with his address book and put us in touch with

00:05:33.426 --> 00:05:35.640
everybody we needed to talk to

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.840
in terms of collecting editorial content,

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.240
in terms of distributing or anything we needed.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.940
As long as we had made an appointment before we turned up,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:51.620
he would then be totally generous with his time.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:06:03.520
And that in itself has influenced me completely during my career

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.520
because I believe one should...

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.880
Every student who rings up and wants half an hour of your time,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.320
you give it because I think that's what he gave to me

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.540
and I think that's something you give back as well.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.700
And so I feel there was huge generosity there,

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.520
a social generosity in that he was a wonderful raconteur

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.220
and party-goer and lunch companion.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:39.800
I worked very closely with Cedric when I was technical editor at AD,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:47.120
where we produced a catalogue, really, of his work up until then.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.220
And, you know, like almost everything he did,

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:55.960
you had to go back to first principles about why you were doing something,

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:57.820
how you actually published it,

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:05.920
and then you did something which was a bit different to a normal way of doing it.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:11.980
All the constraints of publishing were set aside, really.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:18.270
And also, of course, you set aside the idea that he had any fixed ideas about

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:18.780
things,

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:23.277
because you realise that although he may have done a scheme a couple of years

00:07:23.277 --> 00:07:23.680
before,

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:27.100
times would have changed, his own thinking would have changed,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.160
and therefore you had to re-evaluate things

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:33.680
in the context of the time that has elapsed.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.000
So each project that we published,

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.080
there would be comments by him that would bring it up to date

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.860
and also then comments by me about how I read the situation

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:51.260
and how I interpreted the project.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.200
So there was that element to it.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:59.680
Nothing was fixed, and to a certain extent, again,

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:05.520
the internet would have been a much better way of publishing his blogs

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.740
rather than actually paper was not, in terms of publishing,

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:16.720
the thing that would have been best to describe the shifting attitudes

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.980
towards projects he'd only done maybe 12 months before.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:24.820
That being said, he was a great one for collecting paper.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:32.923
he loved indexing there was every sheet of paper that he gave you had a whole

00:08:32.923 --> 00:08:33.660
complex

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:41.843
list of references and so on so that it could easily be found in the finding system

00:08:41.843 --> 00:08:42.040
so

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:46.880
again that was something that you know he would have been very much at home in the

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:50.080
sort of data-driven world we live in today.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:55.780
And so he liked the organisation of it,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:00.320
so instead of publishing it as a story or a long article,

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.500
everything was very episodic,

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:08.500
everything was in a special gridded system that we set it into,

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:13.760
and everything was referenced with its own numbers

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:19.892
so that, again, they could be filed and you could actually tear the centrepiece out

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:21.000
of the magazine

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.420
and you could actually file it into one whole publication.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:35.592
Poliart was the one I got most involved with in that I totally understood his

00:09:35.592 --> 00:09:36.780
frustration

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:42.580
at the sort of skills available in education across the country,

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:48.420
but the inability of students within each school to access those skills available.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.760
Again, this is something which today would be much easier to do with digital media,

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:01.630
but then in a sort of paper-driven teaching base that we had then, it was more

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:02.580
difficult.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:08.360
So PolyArc was a way of creating a network between schools,

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.380
and we got together the PolyArc bus.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:17.620
The Polyark bus was using a Routemaster.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:24.860
At that time, London Transport had actually just bought a whole lot of new buses

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:30.360
and it had fields full of very good quality Routemaster buses,

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:36.060
which they sold for, I think they were only about £3,000 you could buy one for then.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:42.860
The AA bought one and Tony Gwilliam, who was then second year tutor,

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:53.620
actually had one which the students were converting into larger space at the top

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:56.320
with wings that folded out.

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:00.360
So you create a sort of three times the floor area

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:08.420
and a bathroom pod which fitted into the deck at the back of the bus.

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:16.960
And so we borrowed the bus and Dennis Crompton and the communications unit

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:24.400
set up a whole series of videos for us of mainly AA tutors

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:27.680
and of course Cedric as well,

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:31.100
talking about a series of issues.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.060
So we went with these videos.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:35.040
We got on the bus, drove up.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:36.300
First stop was Cambridge.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:40.660
Then we went on up to Nottingham, Edinburgh and so on.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.240
And in each place we unloaded the video kit,

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:51.140
showed people films and videos of AA tutors mainly.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:56.760
And then we also took videos of the schools that we arrived at

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:01.560
to get their responses to some of the things they'd seen

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.300
and also to take messages from one school onto the next.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:11.600
And so we went up through Nottingham.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:16.500
Nottingham was a slight problem in that we hit a low bridge,

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:21.480
but luckily the steel structure of the leaves that came out

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:29.260
was so strong that we actually just forced the bus down on its suspension

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:35.500
and skidded under the bridge, but it did knock some of the construction off.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:43.580
So it wasn't all plain sailing, but it was very instructive

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:49.360
for students who we picked up and then took on to the next school.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:54.002
So we would pick Cambridge students up, take them to Nottingham, leave them there

00:12:54.002 --> 00:12:57.040
and then Nottingham students went on up to Newcastle.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:05.188
I remember one of the, we took Eric Parry from Newcastle up to Edinburgh and then we

00:13:05.188 --> 00:13:08.440
actually got thrown out of Edinburgh

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:14.287
because one of the AA students who was something of a radical was offended by the

00:13:14.287 --> 00:13:17.680
fact that we were not able to use their studios

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:22.300
because there was a dance event going on,

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:23.980
and he was Glaswegian,

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.420
and there was some tension between Glasgow students

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:28.620
and Edinburgh students.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:32.900
We were persona non grata in Edinburgh at the time,

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:37.180
although that was where I first met Diane Sujic,

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:39.880
who then later had become editor of Blueprint.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:44.960
Then we actually went down to Bath

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:51.280
and met a chap who was a great friend of Cedric's.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.240
Again, one of these people he'd introduced us to

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:58.560
who had a wonderful Gothic mansion he was restoring

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:05.780
and had the main concession for repairing Morris Miners in Bath.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.540
Charlie Ware, his name was, who was a great friend of Cedric's.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:14.060
So we went down to Bath, Bristol, then back up to London again.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:21.160
So polyarch was then very physical, now would be more digital,

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:28.660
and in fact there is still a sort of polyarch network that continues on today,

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.320
exchanging information about school.

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:38.742
So polyarch was interesting and we produced a big chunk of an issue of AD on

00:14:38.742 --> 00:14:39.680
polyarch.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:45.400
Potteries Thinkbelt he was working on that while I was here at the AA and again

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:52.480
you know that continued on all sorts of issues I was interested in which

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:59.260
about temporariness about change about reuse about sustainability

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:06.581
and it brought together so many issues which I think you know have grown in

00:15:06.581 --> 00:15:08.240
significance over

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:12.860
the years but which were totally ignored by most of the people teaching architecture

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:13.520
at the time.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:24.280
Yes, the Potteries Thinkbelt was a project related to the area around Stoke-on-Trent

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:32.700
which had been an area of great industrial growth in the 19th, early 20th century

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:36.420
where pottery was made.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.580
It's an area that Cedric knew well.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:42.360
There was massive unemployment

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:46.940
and the area was in need of regeneration.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:51.440
And so he saw education,

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:53.920
the economic salvation of the area,

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:57.540
the knowledge economy, which we know about today,

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:01.500
but was then not something people even spoke about.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:04.800
So, you know, Frederick was incredibly prescient, really,

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:07.660
in realising that this was a key driver

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:11.080
for economic growth and regeneration.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:17.380
And then, again, very different to the normal reaction

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:19.920
in those days of you just build buildings

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:22.420
to create regenerative opportunities,

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:24.980
he actually used the existing infrastructure,

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.120
redundant rail carriages and so on,

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:30.720
could be moved around, were very flexible,

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:36.720
they made ideal teaching spaces and so the architecture actually became the

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:40.740
network the connection between the main centers within the Potteries Thinkbelt

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:45.780
area which could reuse that infrastructure to deliver a totally

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:54.980
different sort of economy Cedric was influential because he was very canny a

00:16:54.980 --> 00:17:04.680
amazing networker and he knew which buttons to press to actually have an

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:10.720
influence on things he was he was very close to senior members of the Labour

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:18.720
Party for instance and he tended to keep a lot of these areas slightly in silos

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:24.000
you didn't meet a lot of the people he mixed with and they party every now and

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:31.402
then you'd bump into them in East Grinstead. But so he, within the Labour movement,

00:17:31.402 --> 00:17:31.580
he

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:37.634
had a whole network of people. So if he needed to put a bit of pressure in high

00:17:37.634 --> 00:17:38.400
places, he

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:48.040
knew how to do that. He had a very strong social network. He moved in, I mean, just

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:51.780
his connections with Tony Snowdon and people like that,

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:58.780
the fact that he was a regular visitor to Kensington Palace

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:01.680
at the time when Princess Margaret was there

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:06.040
was again a link into a wider society.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:12.500
Yet then when Prince Charles spoke out about architecture

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:15.860
and was promoting an idea of, I might say,

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:19.700
the antithesis to what Cedric thought the future of architecture was,

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:21.920
he got very upset about that.

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:28.900
And I did actually organise a dinner for the architecture club,

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.420
of which I was secretary at the time,

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:34.060
which Cedric was a long-standing member of the architecture club.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:38.680
He had been a member of the architecture before I was even secretary.

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:42.020
And he would turn up to almost every dinner.

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:46.520
generally in his cups at the end of the dinner

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:51.280
would talk at great length on some esoteric subject

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:56.720
but once we invited the Prince of Wales

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:59.080
to speak at dinner

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.860
Cedric never came to another architecture club dinner after that

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.580
he was really annoyed and insulted

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.380
that we had actually given a platform to the Prince of Wales

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:12.280
so although he moved within those circles

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:14.440
he knew where to draw the line

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:19.440
he was a very social animal

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:25.280
so that he was all around the place

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:27.740
he would be at all the parties

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:31.060
so he knew how to network in a very sophisticated way

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:35.260
and he knew how to use the press as well

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:38.860
he was obviously great friends with Rainer Bannum

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:41.340
when he was writing in New Society

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:45.360
and also with Paul Barker who was editor of New Society

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:49.440
then New Society at that time was a very powerful magazine

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:53.720
and articles like his Non-Plan article

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:58.040
really can have resonance to this day

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:04.480
so he knew how to communicate in that sort of sphere

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:12.958
I used to criticise him really for, he had a fairly obtuse way of using English

00:20:12.958 --> 00:20:21.758
which he thought was fine because he felt people needed to make a bit of an effort

00:20:21.758 --> 00:20:25.300
to understand what he was saying.

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:31.480
however I do think that did restrict sometimes the breadth of the audience

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:39.100
that he could have garnered if he had really tried. Cedric was about people and

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:44.140
the way that people relate to architecture and his well-known

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:50.180
aphorism that you know when asked about a new house for a couple he said you

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:56.418
don't want a house you need a divorce is something which I'm sure many other people

00:20:56.418 --> 00:20:57.620
have repeated to

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:03.860
you but I think nevertheless shows that you actually obtain the solutions that

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:04.820
aren't often

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:13.620
expected of buildings through other means that we are dealing with the way people

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:22.080
lead their lives and the architects knee-jerk response is too often just to

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:29.460
build something to house whatever activity that is whereas he showed us

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:34.260
that there are ways that don't require building that you can actually have the

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:42.100
same impact and I would say that if you look at interaction and what happened

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:50.180
and the fact that meanwhile spaces, pop-ups, temporary use,

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:56.160
all those sorts of things have an impact on the way that cities work

00:21:56.160 --> 00:22:00.760
without having to be fixed buildings like the ones around us here.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:08.060
That influence has been very powerful and I think has shown to be effective.

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:14.571
I think the relevance of Cedric today is actually almost more important because

00:22:14.571 --> 00:22:21.000
although he is a powerful figure in the architectural community at the moment,

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:28.623
I feel that we are going through a period where architects are actually producing

00:22:28.623 --> 00:22:35.400
more fixed pieces of iconic architecture than at any previous period really.

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:39.840
and that is the antithesis of what Cedric was about.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:46.880
And I would say that if you look at the world today

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:49.220
in terms of architectural output,

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:54.160
we are building a huge volume of space

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:58.280
that is so constrained by design

00:22:58.280 --> 00:23:00.840
that actually we're going to find it very difficult

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:04.980
to actually operate those pieces of kit in the future.

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:11.020
and that we need to actually look at a much freer way of building

00:23:11.020 --> 00:23:15.520
and other contemporaries of Cedric like Habrakon for instance

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:21.060
need to be brought into the mix

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:26.940
to look at systems of building that provide greater freedom

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:31.400
and actually look more at the use than the artifact.
